    "case_id": "537",
    "case_summary": "A 19-year-old female student (weight: 35 kg, height: 159 cm) was admitted with grade III dyspnea. She was prescribed diuretics (spironolactone–torsemide combination) and aspirin post-surgery. After starting a new medication at low dose (25 mg per day divided into two doses) for two days, the dose was increased to 25 mg twice daily on the third day. She was discharged on the fifth postoperative day but was readmitted the same evening.\n\nThe patient presented with bouts of an extremely stiff, nonpainful neck that tilted her head involuntarily leftwards, upwards, and backwards in hyperextension. The patient exhibited:- Conjugate deviation of the eyes upward or laterally- Involuntary posturing with head, neck, and spine arched backward- Difficulty in opening or closing the mouth\n\n- Initial Cardiac Assessment:  - Echocardiographic evaluation showed subpulmonic ventricular septal defect, severe aortic regurgitation due to right coronary cusp prolapse, and dilated left ventricle with ejection fraction of 40%  - Post-repair transesophageal echocardiographic assessment showed mild aortic regurgitation- Laboratory Tests:  - Blood chemistry and electrolyte levels were within normal limits- Imaging Evidence:  Image Title: The head tilted and neck in hyperextension  Image Description: Patient showing dystonic posturing of head and neck    Video Title: Movement of the head and neck with unusual posturing  Video Description: Shows abnormal movement and posturing (Duration: 0:26)",
